Z||000004||WisconsinGT||02-23-2000||10:49 PM||||Is there a trick for setting the timing on a 2.8? I hear that you should advance the timing 1. Your car should run alot better. Does anyone know how to do this? If so, can you share whit all of us? Also, I need to tape some wires together near the engine. What should I use? Electrical Tape?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Phaeton||02-23-2000||11:58 PM||||W-GT, those wires by the engine should be encased in plastic conduit, its really inexpensive and won't make a gooey mess when it's time to take the wires apart again.<BR>The prettier engine compartment is a bonus.<BR>Parts stores carry it, use the smallest diameter that fully encloses the wires.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Tom88gt||02-24-2000||09:35 AM||||Each notch is indeed 2 degrees.  Pretty much a universal standard.  Clean the plate so you can find TDC.  Should be a conspicuous mark.<P>As for advancing the stock setting.  Yes this will work to some degree.  But you must consider the fact that piston will be traveling up longer while the flame front will be moving towards it.  When they contact you have detonation.  Plus, lower octane gas lowers the flash point, increasing combustion speed and the chance for detonation.<P>Anytime you alter the stock setting or modify the engine, its an experiment.  Make sure you thought of everything.    ||||reg||1
Z||000007||penetrator||02-24-2000||10:46 AM||||About the timing, i dont understand much. <BR>The only thing i know about it, is that you are adjusting the time for the igniton and things like that. But where are thoose marks, and timing light, what's that? And i also heard here in this Forum, that you must ground the ECM before you changing the timing. I did once twisted my Cap and Rotor package(is it distributor), because my car was running like a Truck, so i twisted it just a little bit clockwise til the car sounded right. But i did't grounded my ECM och timinglights? And that probably not good.  Because my car dies when its hot and when im stopping at redlights. But im not sure if it is because i was "fooling" with the ignition stuff. Please, can someone clear this things up for me?  <P>Thanks<BR>Magnus||||reg||1
Z||000008||Oreif||02-24-2000||11:03 AM||||Penetrator, The timing you adjusted is when the spark fires in relation to the stroke of the piston. To set the timing of the engine you need to ground the ECM so that the timing stays stable. the ECM will vary the timing by 4 degrees (+/-2 from what is set)<BR>The timing marks are generally a small plate attached above the crankshaft pully. There is a groove cut into the pulley so you can see where the timing is. Since the engine is running you need a timing light. The timing light is a triggered strobe light. You hook an inductive pick-up type clamp onto the #1 spark plug and the strobe flashes with each firing of the spark plug. This allows you to see where the groove on the pully is in relation to the marks on the timing plate. It sometimes helps to color the groove (engine off) with a piece of chalk to see it better. Typically you want the #1 spark plug to fire 10 degrees before the piston hits the top of the cylinder. That way the spark ignites the fuel/air mixture just as the piston peaks getting the most compression and power. clockwise advances, counter clockwise retards timing. If your retarded too much the engine will knock and stutter at idle if you are too far advanced the engine will knock under acceleration (ping).<BR>Retard = closer to 0 degrees<BR>advanced = farther away from 0 degrees.<P>Does this help?||||reg||1
Z||000009||penetrator||02-24-2000||11:27 AM||||Yes thanks. Know i understand it more. So this is nothig you should't do without a timinglight then. <BR>Is this plate open, or is it covered under something? <P>And could my changes make my car to die when it have reached the working temp? But it only dies when i engages the cluth.<P>Thanks||||reg||10
Z||000010||WisconsinGT||02-24-2000||06:01 PM||||Thanks for your help. But I am hooking up Rodney Dickmans tach filter. And I need to put something around the wires that connect to the plastic piece. I am thinking some kind of tape will do the trick...but which one?||||reg||1
Z||000011||William Federle||02-24-2000||07:36 PM||||I have an 84 SE and I just had to set the timing after replacing the O ring on my distributer. I wiped off the plate with the little tube at the end of it with a rag and looked at it with a flashlight. 8 deg. was the 3rd point from the back of the car. I put a little dab of white paint on it. Then I rotated the engine by rocking the car in gear (it doesn't have a lot of compression) until I saw the notch in the crank-pulley. I put some white paint on that and then wiped it off leaving the notch with white paint in it. Then, after jumpering pins A and B on the ALDL I started the car after hooking up the timing light, noted which side of the stationary mark the one on the pulley was and then stopped the engine and adjusted the distributer. Then I restarted the engine and observed the change. It took about 3 or 4 tries to zero in on 8 deg. ||||reg||1
Z||000012||daved||02-24-2000||09:45 PM||||William, <BR>Your method is o/k, but it is a little easier to slacken the distributor bolt and adjust the timing while watching the timing mark.  Try it, you'll like it! <BR>Don't forget to recheck it after you tighten the bolt to see if it moved..usually not a problem.<BR>Dave||||reg||1
Z||000013||William Federle||02-24-2000||09:50 PM||||I was going to but I thought I'd get zapped.||||reg||1
Z||000014||Brian84SE||02-25-2000||08:43 PM||||Thanks William. So what your saying is 8 degrees is the very last point on the scale (heading towards the rear of the car)||||reg||1
Z||000015||William Federle||02-25-2000||08:58 PM||||No, it's the third from the rear of the car. If you start at the end of the scale nearest the trunk and count three points, the 3rd is  marked "8" on my car. There is at least one more point after that before you get to the end of the scale nearest the firewall.||||reg||1
